-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('50aa5e60-24c7-592a-a197-dd2c65b9665e', 'rg11-context-clues-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Apprentice$q$, $q$Ines Valtonen did not believe in speeches. When Kwame Oduya arrived at her violin workshop for his first day as an apprentice, she handed him an apron, pointed at a cracked instrument on the bench, and said, 'Watch.' That was the entire orientation. Her laconic style unsettled him at first; he had expected rules, warnings, perhaps a tour, and instead he received a single word.

Over the following weeks, Kwame learned that the workshop ran on tacit understandings. Nobody told him that the brushes belonged on the left rack, yet when he set one on the right, Ines silently moved it back. Nobody said that customers were never to be quoted a price until a repair was finished, but he noticed that she always waited. The rules existed; they simply were never spoken aloud.

His first real task was to clean an old violin before Ines repaired its seam. Eager to move on to more interesting work, he gave the instrument a perfunctory wipe, two quick passes with a cloth, and set it aside. Ines picked it up, turned it toward the window, and showed him the film of rosin still clinging near the bridge. She returned it to him without a word.

The second time, he spent nearly an hour on the same violin. He worked slowly around the f-holes and along the edges, where dust gathers in the grooves of the varnish. When he finished, Ines examined it for a long time. Then she nodded, which Kwame had come to understand was the highest praise she gave.

By spring, he could conceive of a future in which he ran a workshop of his own. The idea had seemed absurd in September. Now, as he steamed a warped bridge back into shape, the plan felt less like a fantasy and more like a destination he could actually reach.$q$, 'draft')
