-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e2689ef4-27fd-53dc-9fbe-469045b5c079', 'rg11-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Messages Across the Hills$q$, $q$Long before electric wires, a few governments in the late 1700s tried to speed up communication with chains of towers built on hilltops. Each tower held tall wooden arms that an operator could move into dozens of positions. An operator in the next tower, watching through a telescope, copied each position and passed it along. In clear weather, a short message could cross hundreds of kilometers in less than an hour, a speed that seemed almost miraculous at a time when letters traveled by horse.

The system had obvious drawbacks, and its critics were quick to name them. Fog, rain, and darkness halted every transmission, so the network was useless for much of the year in some regions. Building and staffing the towers was also costly. Critics called the project a luxury no treasury could sustain, yet the officials in charge remained intransigent, refusing every proposal to shut the lines down even after repeated failures.

Supporters argued that the towers proved their value during emergencies, when a delay of days could be disastrous. They also tried to ameliorate the worst problems rather than abandon the system. Engineers placed towers closer together in misty valleys, trained operators to work in shifts, and developed codebooks that shrank long phrases into a few signals, which made each transmission shorter and less prone to error.

The codebooks addressed a second concern as well. Because anyone with a telescope could watch the arms move, the messages were never truly private. To protect sensitive information, officials kept the meaning of each signal arcane, known only to a small group of trained clerks. An ordinary observer could see every movement and still have no idea what was being said.

Within a few decades, the electric telegraph made the towers obsolete. A wire could carry messages day and night, in any weather, and the old hilltop lines were no longer needed. Many towers were abandoned, though a few still stand, quiet reminders of an ambitious idea.$q$, 'draft')
