-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('546e9dff-3bc6-5849-9d93-5f92ab64d76e', 'rg11-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Fog at Kettle Ridge$q$, $q$The fog at Kettle Ridge was so thick that morning that Priya Ramaswamy could barely see the orange flags marking the course. Coach Dagher gathered the team at the starting line and gave his usual advice. 'Run your own race,' he said. 'Do not chase anyone in the first mile.' Priya had heard the same words before every meet for three years, and she nodded along, half listening.

Tomasz Novak, a freshman running his first varsity race, was far less composed. He bounced on his toes, retied his shoes twice, and kept glancing at the older runners. Priya remembered feeling that same jittery dread before her own first race, when every small sound made her jump.

When the starter's signal sounded, Tomasz sprinted to the front. Priya winced. His start was impetuous, driven by nerves rather than any plan, and she knew what usually followed such a rash decision. By the second mile, he had faded badly, his stride short and ragged.

As she caught up to him, Priya slowed for a moment. 'Stay with me,' she said. 'Breathe on my count.' He fell in at her shoulder, and together they settled into a steady rhythm. The pace felt almost leisurely compared to his frantic opening burst, but it was one they could hold until the finish.

They crossed the line in the middle of the pack, nowhere near a medal. Tomasz looked crestfallen, his shoulders slumped and his eyes fixed on the ground. Priya told him that nearly every runner on the team had made the same mistake in a first race. Coach Dagher was even more sanguine. 'You finished strong,' he said, smiling. 'The rest is practice.' On the bus home, Tomasz asked Priya to explain how she paced the hills, and she realized the coach's familiar advice had never made more sense to her.$q$, 'draft')
