-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('05050e51-5404-579d-af75-41336f95f769', 'rg11-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Wheel by the Creek$q$, $q$The Marlow Historical Society occupied the upper floor of a venerable brick building that had served as the town's post office for more than a century. Its stairs creaked, its windows rattled in the wind, and its shelves held boxes that no one had opened in decades. When Lucia Benavides volunteered there for the summer, the archivist, Mr. Sato, assigned her a single box of letters and a stack of blank catalog cards.

Ostensibly, the job was simple: read each letter, record the date and the names, and file the card. In practice, it was nothing of the kind. The handwriting was faded, the dates were often missing, and several writers referred to people only by nicknames. Lucia soon realized that a cursory glance at each page would tell her almost nothing, so she began reading every letter twice, slowly, with a magnifying glass.

Her assiduous approach paid off in July. While comparing two letters written decades apart, she noticed the same unusual phrase, a reference to 'the wheel by the creek.' Mr. Sato explained that the town had once had a mill there, though no photographs of it survived. Lucia's initial conception of the mill was a small wooden shed. After she found a third letter describing a stone building three stories tall, she had to revise that picture entirely.

By August, she had gathered enough details to sketch a rough map of the old mill site. Mr. Sato studied it for a long time. 'It is conceivable,' he said, 'that the foundation is still under the parking lot behind the library.' He did not promise anything, but he agreed to share her map with the town's planning office.

Lucia left the society that fall with a new respect for faded paper. A box that had looked dull in June had turned into the most interesting puzzle of her year.$q$, 'draft')
